.TH LOCALPASSKEY-GUI 1 "October 2026" "localpasskey 0.4.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
localpasskey-gui \- window for browsing, restoring and removing the passkeys and authenticator codes stored on this computer
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B localpasskey-gui
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B localpasskey-gui
opens a window listing the passkeys that
.BR localpasskeyd (8)
keeps for you, one per website account, in the QDarkStyle Qt theme also used
by Electrum and Feather Wallet. It is in the applications menu as
.BR LocalPasskey .
.PP
Selecting a passkey shows its website, account, whether it is a passkey
(signs you in without a username) or a security key (a second step after your
password), its key algorithm, and when it was created and last used. From
there, the Passkey menu or a right-click, you can copy the username, open the
website, or move the passkey to the trash. Moving to the trash asks for
confirmation, and then for your password.
.PP
The list takes several rows at once: click with Shift to extend the selection
or with Ctrl to add a row, and Ctrl+A selects them all.
.B Move to Trash\[u2026]
and
.B Restore
then act on every selected row after one confirmation, sent to the service as
one request, and the side panel says how many are selected.
.PP
A passkey in the trash stops working but is not destroyed. Besides
.B Move to Trash\[u2026]
for the selected passkey, the Passkey menu has
.B Move All for This Website to Trash\[u2026]
and
.BR "Move All Passkeys to Trash\[u2026]" .
.B Show Trash
in the View menu switches the list to the trash, where the third column shows
when each passkey was moved there, and the window offers
.BR Restore ,
.B Restore All for This Website
and
.BR "Restore All" .
Bulk actions say how many passkeys they affect and ask before they act.
Nothing in the trash expires.
.B Delete Permanently\[u2026]
removes one passkey for good: type the site name to enable its button.
This cannot be undone. Remove passkeys from your account settings on the
website as well.
.SH AUTHENTICATOR CODES
The
.B Authenticator Codes
tab next to
.B Passkeys
lists the one-time codes that the service keeps for sites that ask for one as
a second step: time-based ones (TOTP), Steam Guard's five characters, and
counter-based ones (HOTP), whose codes must be used in order. They complement
passkeys and do not replace them: a fake site can ask for a code and pass it
on, which it cannot do with a passkey. Opening the tab asks for your password
once (polkit action
.BR org.localpasskey.totp );
until then it shows a locked message with an
.B Unlock
button. Once unlocked, the list shows the current code of each entry, which
changes by itself, and the details show it in large digits with a countdown
and a
.B Copy Code
button. Copied codes are removed from the clipboard after 30 seconds if they
are still there. An HOTP code has no countdown and no code until you ask: its
details show the counter the next code will use instead.
.B Next Code
in the details, or in the Code menu, asks the service for that code and moves
the counter on. Showing the codes never uses a counter, and neither does
copying the code shown. Codes are hidden while the window is in the
background. The tab locks again when you leave it or close the window. It also
locks once the window has asked for no code for 5 minutes, for example while
in the background, and within a few seconds of the service dropping the unlock
(when it restarts, for example), also while the trash is shown; the tab then
shows
.B Unlock
again.
.PP
In this tab the Code menu, also on right-click, replaces the Passkey menu;
a right-click on the empty space below the rows offers
.BR "Add Code\[u2026]" .
The list takes several rows at once, as the passkey list does, and
.B Move to Trash\[u2026]
and
.B Restore
act on all of them. Double-clicking a row, or the large code in the details,
copies that code as
.B Copy Code
does.
.B Add Code\[u2026]
takes the
.B otpauth://totp/
link, an
.B otpauth://hotp/
link with its
.B counter
parameter, a
.B steam://
link or a link with
.BR encoder=steam ,
or the setup key that the site shows next to its QR code; type it or
use
.BR Paste ,
which reads the clipboard only when clicked. The dialog checks it as you
type, fills in the issuer and account from a link, and suggests the
website. A full address typed as the website, such as
.BR https://github.com/login ,
is saved as its host name, and so is one with a path or port such as
.BR github.com:443 .
A Google Authenticator export link
.RB ( otpauth-migration:// ,
from
.I Transfer accounts
\(->
.IR "Export accounts" )
is read too: the dialog says how many codes it holds and why any are skipped
(for example, MD5-based ones), and
.B Save
imports them all with a single confirmation. After saving, the window clears
the clipboard if it still holds the pasted text. The key field hides what you
type unless you click
.BR Show ;
the window never shows or copies a saved setup key.
.B Edit\[u2026]
changes the website, issuer and account.
.BR "Move to Trash\[u2026]" ,
.B Show Trash
and, in the trash,
.B Restore
and
.B Delete Permanently\[u2026]
work as for passkeys; permanent deletion asks you to type the site, the issuer
when there is no site, or the word
.B delete
when there is neither, and always asks for your password. Once you have
unlocked the tab with your password, the other changes do not ask for it
again until the tab locks.
.PP
.B Settings\[u2026]
in the File menu holds
.BR "Ask for my password before showing authenticator codes" ,
on by default. Turning it off asks for your password; turning it on does
not.
.SH THE PIN
Browsers ask for a PIN in their security-key settings before they list or
delete the passkeys of a security key.
.B Security Key PIN\[u2026]
in the File menu, or a click on the lock at the right of the status bar, shows
whether a security key PIN is set and how many attempts are left, and sets a
new one, changes it or removes it. The lock is open when no PIN is set,
closed when one is, and red when it is locked after too many wrong attempts;
its tooltip says which. The PIN is not your login password and is never sent
to websites; if you forget it, set a new one. Only a hash of the PIN is
stored, and the PIN alone never deletes a passkey: every passkey change in
the window still asks for your password. The command-line equivalent is
.B localpasskey pin
in
.BR localpasskey (1).
.SH BACKUPS
.B Export Passkeys\[u2026]
in the File menu writes an encrypted LocalPasskey backup of all your
passkeys, or of those for the selected website. Choose a new backup
passphrase and confirm it; it encrypts the file and is not your login
password. The line below the fields says what is still wrong with it, and
Export stays disabled until it is at least 8 characters long and both fields
match. After you choose where to save the file (readable by you only), the
service asks for your login password to allow the export. Whoever has the file and the passphrase can sign in as
you. The window never writes unencrypted files; see
.B localpasskey export \-\-format cxf
in
.BR localpasskey (1)
for that.
.PP
In the export dialog,
.BI "Include authenticator codes (" N )
adds all your
.I N
live authenticator codes with their setup keys to the backup when you export
all passkeys. When you export one website's passkeys it reads
.B Include this website's authenticator codes
and adds only the codes saved for that website. It is checked by default
when you have authenticator codes, and greyed out when you have none.
A backup with codes cannot be opened by LocalPasskey 0.3.1 or earlier.
.PP
.B Import Passkeys\[u2026]
opens a LocalPasskey backup, a CXF file or an unencrypted Bitwarden JSON
export. It asks for the passphrase only for a backup, then lists what will be
imported and what is skipped and why: already on this computer (also in the
trash), another passkey for the same account, or twice in the file.
Authenticator codes in a backup are listed and imported with the passkeys.
.B Import
then asks for your password.
.SH KEYBOARD SHORTCUTS
.TP
Ctrl+F
Search by website or account; Escape clears the search.
.TP
Ctrl+T
Show or hide the trash.
.TP
Ctrl+C
Copy the username of the selected passkey, or the current code of the
selected authenticator code.
.TP
Ctrl+N
Add an authenticator code (in the Authenticator Codes tab).
.TP
Delete
Move the selected passkey or authenticator code to the trash; with several
rows selected, all of them, after one confirmation.
.TP
F5
Refresh the list.
.TP
Ctrl+Q
Quit.
.PP
The list follows the service: passkeys you create in a browser appear within
a few seconds.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR localpasskey (1),
.BR localpasskeyd (8)
